<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · War and march · c. 1218 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Palästinalied (Palestine Song)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="gmh">Palästinalied</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song from about 800 years ago by the German poet Walther, about a pilgrim who reaches the Holy Land.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Walther von der Vogelweide</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Walther von der Vogelweide (melody possibly a contrafactum of Jaufre Rudel's Lanquan li jorn)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German crusaders and pilgrims; the Middle High German courtly poets</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>German lands of the Holy Roman Empire (exact place not known)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 13th century, at the time of the Fifth Crusade (1217-1221)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Walther von der Vogelweide (early 13th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palastinalied">Palästinalied</a> (&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;) is a song written in the early 13th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a>, the most celebrated lyric poet of Middle High German literature. It was written at the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> (1217-1221). It is one of the few songs by Walther for which a melody has survived, and that melody has been suggested to be a contrafactum, a new text set to an existing tune, of the 12th-century troubadour Jaufre Rudel's song Lanquan li jorn.</p><p>The song tells the Christian gospel from the point of view of a pilgrim setting foot in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. Its closing stanza turns to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> themselves: it notes that Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Muslims (the &quot;heathens&quot; of the text) all claim the land, and declares the Christian claim the just one, asking God to grant it.</p><p>The words survive in several medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> that do not agree on their length. The oldest is the Kleine Heidelberger Liederhandschrift (manuscript A, c. 1270), with seven stanzas; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a> (manuscript C, c. 1304) has nine, and other manuscripts add four more, for thirteen distinct stanzas in all. One of these is clearly later than the original, and scholars do not agree whether the other five not found in manuscript A are Walther's. The melody is first written down in the Münster fragment (manuscript Z), about a century after Walther's death. The Carmina Burana manuscript (c. 1230) already gives the first stanza with neumes, though too few to rebuild the tune, as an appendix to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> song Alte clamat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a>, which was to be sung to the same melody.</p><p>From the 1970s, and especially with the rise of medieval rock, neofolk and related styles in the late 1980s and 1990s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palastinalied">Palästinalied</a> became a staple of such music and is now well known to modern audiences, chiefly through German bands such as Ougenweide (1974), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvus">Corvus</a> Corax (1991), Qntal (1995), Estampie (1996) and In Extremo (1998). A 2002 compilation, Palästinalied-Projekt, gathers twenty performances, and the first movement of Lou Harrison's String Quartet Set takes much of its melody from the song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song speaks as a pilgrim who has at last reached the land where Christ lived, and it retells the gospel story as bound to that land. Its mood is of awe and fulfilment. Its close moves from devotion to the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a>, asserting the Christian right to the land against the other faiths that claim it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Middle High German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Middle High German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Alrerst lebe ich mir werde· sit min sv́ndig ǒge siht·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Only now do I live in worth in my own eyes, since my sinful eye sees</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dc reine lant vnd ǒch die erde·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the pure land and the soil as well</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">der man so vil eren giht·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to which people ascribe so much honour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">es ist geschehen des ich ie bat·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What I always prayed for has come to pass:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">ich bin komen an die stat· da got menschlichen trat·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have come to the place where God walked in human form.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Schoͤnv́ lant rich vnd here·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Beautiful lands, rich and splendid,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">swc ich der noch han gesehen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whatever of them I have seen so far,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">so bist dvs ir aller ere·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you are the honour of them all.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wc ist wunders hie geschehen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What a wonder has happened here!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">das ein magt ein kint gebar·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That a maiden bore a child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">here vber aller engel schar·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lord over all the host of angels:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wc das niht ein wunder gar·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>was that not a wonder entirely?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Hie lies er sich reine tǒfen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here he, the pure one, let himself be baptised,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dc der mensche reine si·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that mankind might be pure.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">do lies er sich hie verkǒfen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then he let himself be sold here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dc wir eigen wurden fri·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that we who were bondsmen became free.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">anders weren wir verlorn·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Otherwise we would have been lost.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wol dir sper crúce vnd dorn·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail to you, spear, cross and thorn!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wie dir ze den ist din zorn·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[this line is unclear in the manuscript]</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 67 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Do er sich wolte vber vns erbarmen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When he wished to have mercy on us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">do leit er den grimmen tot·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then he suffered the cruel death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">er vil rich vber vns vil armenn·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he, so mighty, for us who were so poor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dc wir komen vs der not· dc in do des niht verdros·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that we might come out of our distress. That it did not irk him then,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dast ein wunder al zegros· aller wunder vber genos·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that is a wonder far too great, surpassing all wonders.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Hinnen fůr der svn zehelle·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From here the Son went to hell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">von dem grabe dâr inne lac·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the grave in which he lay.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dest der vanter ie geselle·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this the Father was ever his companion [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnd der geist den nieman mac·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the Spirit, whom no one can</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">svnder scheiden es si ein·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>set apart: it is one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">sleht vnd ebener danne ein zein·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>straight and smoother than an arrow-shaft,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">als er abrahame erschein·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as he appeared to Abraham.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Do er den tievel also geschande·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When he had so put the devil to shame</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">das nie keiser bas gestreit·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that no emperor ever fought better,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">do fůr er her wider zelande·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>then he came back here to the land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">do hv̊b sich do der ivden leit·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then the grief of the Jews began:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dc er herre ir hv̊te brach·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that he, the lord, broke through their guard,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnd dc man in sit lebendig sach·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and that he was seen alive afterwards,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">den ir hant slůg vnd stach·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he whom their hand struck and pierced.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">IN dc lant hat er gesprochen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this land he has pronounced</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">einen angeslichen tac· da der weise wirt gerochen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a fearful day, when the orphan will be avenged</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnd dv́ witwe klagen mag·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the widow may bring her complaint,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnd der arme den gewalt· den man hat mit in gestalt·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the poor man the violence that was done to them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wol im dort der hie vergalt·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Well for him there who made amends here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Vnserre lantrehter tihten· vristet da niemans klage·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our local judges' scheming delays no one's suit there,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">wan er wil da ze stunt rihten·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for he will judge there at once:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">so ist es an dem lesten tage·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so it is on the last day.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnd swer deheine schulde hie lat·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And whoever leaves any debt here</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vnverebent [126v] wie der stat·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>unsettled, how will he fare</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dort da er pfant noch búrgen hat·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there, where he has neither pledge nor guarantor?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Ir lat v́ch nit verdriesen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not let it weary you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">das ich noch gesprochen han·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what I have said so far;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">so wil ich die rede entsliêssen·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now I will unlock the matter</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">kvrzwilen vnd ǒch wissen lan·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>briefly, and also make it known:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">swas got mit der welte ie begie·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whatever God ever did with the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">das hv̊b sich dort vnd endet hie·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that began there and ends here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Dar nach was er in deme lande·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>After that he was in the land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">vierzig tage do fv̊r er dar· in sin vater sande·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>forty days; then he went there, whence his Father sent him.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">sinen geist er v́ns bewar·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His Spirit, may he keep us:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">den sant er hin wider zehant·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that he sent back at once.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">heilig ist das selbe lant·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy is that same land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">sin name der ist vor got erkant·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its name is known before God.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">Kristen ivden vnd die heiden·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christians, Jews and the heathens</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">iehent dc dis ir erbe si·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>say that this is their inheritance.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">got mvͤsse es zerehte scheiden·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May God judge it rightly</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">dur die sine namen dri·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by his three names.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">al dú welt stritet her· wir sin an der rehten ger·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the world fights here; we are in the right claim.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="gmh">reht ist dc er vns gewer·</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is right that he grant it to us.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Walther von der Vogelweide (c. 1220); transcribed from a medieval manuscript. Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Alrerst_lebe_ich_mir_werde" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (13)</summary><ul><li>The text is a diplomatic transcription of one manuscript (folio change [126v] in line 8.5 is left out of the translation); its wording differs in places from the usual critical editions, and the translation follows what this manuscript has.</li><li>line 3.7: 'wie dir ze den ist din zorn' does not construe as written; it looks like a corrupt copy of a line cursing the heathens ('woe to you, heathens, that grieves you'). Rendered with [?].</li><li>line 5.3: 'dest der vanter ie geselle' is corrupt ('vanter' for 'vater'); read as 'in this the Father was ever his companion', i.e. the Father did not leave the Son in the harrowing of hell. Uncertain.</li><li>line 5.6: 'zein' is a rod or arrow-shaft; the image (the Trinity as one, straighter and smoother than a shaft) is the poem's own.</li><li>line 1.1: 'mir werde' = 'worthy to myself', i.e. my life now has worth in my own eyes.</li><li>line 3.4: 'eigen' = unfree people, bondsmen (as opposed to 'fri', free).</li><li>line 7.2: 'weise' = orphan (MHG weise), not 'wise'.</li><li>line 8.1: 'lantrehter tihten' read as 'the scheming/devising of our local judges'; the sense is that earthly courts' delays count for nothing at the Last Judgement. 'vristet' = postpones.</li><li>line 8.4: 'schulde' covers both debt and guilt.</li><li>line 9.6: this manuscript has 'dort ... hie' ('began there and ends here'); the sense is that God's dealings with the world began and end in this land.</li><li>line 10.3: 'er vns bewar' read as a wish, 'may he keep us'; other copies have a relative clause ('who may keep us').</li><li>line 11.4: 'by his three names' = by the Trinity.</li><li>line 11.5: 'ger' = desire or claim (also a spear); read as 'we are in the right in our claim'.</li></ul></details></section>
